  {
   "id": "B-31",
   "name": "AlphaProof Code (code-verification)",
   "kind": "system",
   "org": "Google DeepMind",
   "first_public_date": "2026-11-10",
   "date_basis": "DeepMind blog date; repository initial commit 2026-11-10 08:14 UTC",
   "primary_urls": [
    "https://deepmind.google/blog/introducing-alphaproof-code/",
    "https://github.com/google-deepmind/code-verification",
    "https://google-deepmind.github.io/code-verification/"
   ],
   "areas": [
    "formal verification",
    "cryptographic protocols",
    "Lean"
   ],
   "verification_mode": "Lean developments (computational and symbolic) with axiom-audit and trusted-closure scripts; the atlas has not rebuilt the kernel check",
   "atlas_nodes_touched": [],
   "note": "The blog (2026-11-10) says Gemini Carbon agents wrote over six million lines of Lean about Signal's compiled code. The README describes two AI-generated proofs for an AArch64 Linux libsignal-cli build in a CLI harness with hooked cryptographic primitives (libsignal v0.105.4). A static line count on commit 808b4b7 found 7,528,293 physical newlines across 11,323 Lean files, consistent with 'over six million' and not with secondary 'three million' figures. DeepMind's paper says 37 hooked routines (23 cryptographic) are replaced by contracts that 'are not proved against the real routines,' and that the results concern the CLI build, not Signal's own applications. Carry-over to Android and iOS is an extra trust assumption. The atlas has not run lake build."
  },
